                "evidence": "review changes_requested: duetflow/spotify.py:203 (RISKY FILE) \u2014 the 429 branch sleeps for the raw `Retry-After` value with no upper bound, and the retry loop repeats that wait up to MAX_ATTEMPTS-1 times. Verified by probe: a transport raising HttpStatus(429, retry_after=86400) produced sleeps [86400, 86400, 86400, 86400] = 345600 s before the final SpotifyError, i.e. `duetflow collect` blocks for up to four days inside one invocation. Spotify returns multi-hour Retry-After values under sustained rate limiting. Because docs/PLAN.md \u00a78 runs Collection as an hourly systemd `oneshot` (Type=oneshot has no TimeoutStartSec by default), the wedged run suppresses every subsequent hourly Collection, and \u00a75's 50-item recently-played window then silently discards the plays that accumulate meanwhile \u2014 exactly the data loss `warn_if_gap` was added to detect, but the warning cannot fire because the process is still blocked. \u00a75 requires honouring Retry-After, not honouring it without bound: cap the wait (and fail fast past the cap) so a single 429 cannot outlive the collection interval. The inline comment at duetflow/spotify.py:202 and the test `test_429_honors_retry_after_longer_than_two_minutes` (tests/test_collector.py) deliberately lock the uncapped behaviour in, so both need revisiting.; duetflow/collector.py:81-84 \u2014 `_record_run` executes `INSERT INTO runs (...)` directly. docs/PLAN.md \u00a72 states as a hard rule that \"only `db.py` performs SQL\", and this is the only raw SQL left in non-test production code outside db.py (verified: `grep -rn 'conn.execute|executescript|INSERT|SELECT ' duetflow/*.py` outside db.py matches only these lines). The same change already added `last_play_at`, `last_successful_collection` and `record_play` to db.py, so the pattern exists and was simply not followed here. Concrete consequence: the sole writer of the `runs` table lives outside the module that owns the schema, so when phase 06 populates `runs.turnover` the column list at duetflow/collector",
